%N A399406 Order array of the array W given by w(n, k) = k*(n + e), for n >= 1, k >= 1, a rectangular array, read by descending antidiagonals; see Comments.
%C A399406 Suppose that ((n, k)), for n >= 1 and k >= 1 is an array of distinct real numbers. Replace each w(n, k) by its position when all the numbers w(n, k) are ordered by <. The resulting array is the order array of (w(n, k)). Every positive integer occurs exactly once, so that as a sequence this is a permutation of the positive integers. Successive row terms, and column terms, have limiting ratio 1.
%C A399406 Guide to order arrays of arrays of the form (w(n, k)) = (k*n + x), where x is a positive irrational number:
%C A399406 x                   array
%C A399406 ---------------------------------------------
%C A399406 e                   A399406 = (1,5,2,11,8,3,9,...)
%C A399406 sqrt(2)             A399407 = (1,4,2,8,7,3,12,...)
%C A399406 sqrt(3)             A399408 = (1,4,2,9,7,3,14,...)
%C A399406 sqrt(5)             A399408 = (1,5,2,10,8,3,17,...)
%C A399406 sqrt(1/2)           A399409 = (1,3,2,6,7,4,10,...)
%C A399406 sqrt(1/3)           A399410 = (1,3,2,6,7,4,9,...)
%C A399406 sqrt(2) + sqrt(3)   A399411 = (1,6,2,13,9,3,21,...)
%C A399406 sqrt(3) - sqrt(2)   A399412 = (1,3,2,5,7,4,8,...)
%C A399406 2*e                 A399413 = (1,8,2,18,11,3 31,...)
%C A399406 Pi/2                A399414 = (1,4,2,9,7,3,13,...)
%C A399406 Pi                  A399415 = (1,6,2,13,9,3,21,...)
%C A399406 log 2               A399416 = (1,3,2,6,7,9,10,...)
%C A399406 2*Pi                A399418 = (1,9,2,20,12,3,34,...)
%H A399406 <a href="/index/Per#IntegerPermutation">Index entries for sequences that are permutations of the natural numbers</a>
%e A399406 Corner of array (w(n,k)) (approximations):
